1 NOTE Ptolemy VII (184?-116 bc), called Ptolemy Euergetes ("benefactor") II, 
2 CONT king of Egypt (145-116 bc), the son of Ptolemy V and the brother of 
2 CONT Ptolemy VI. He was portrayed by Greek writers as a cruel despot, but 
2 CONT Egyptian writings credit him with administrative reforms and the liberal 
2 CONT endowment of religious institutions. The Ptolemaic Empire became 
2 CONT permanently disunited after his death. His will bequeathed Cyrenaica to 
2 CONT his illegitimate son Ptolemy Apion (died 96 bc) and Egypt and Cyprus to 
2 CONT his second wife Cleopatra III, who was instructed to choose one of her 
2 CONT sons as joint ruler.
