1 NOTE He was the ruler of Syria from 146 BC to 125 BC.
1 NOTE Demetrius II (of Syria), called Nicator (died about 125 bc), king of Syria 
2 CONT (145-139 bc, 127-125 bc), son of Demetrius I. With the aid of Ptolemy VI, 
2 CONT king of Egypt, he deposed and killed the usurper Alexander Balas (reigned 
2 CONT 150-145 bc) and became king of Syria in 145 bc. Demetrius married his 
2 CONT predecessor's widow, Cleopatra Thea (died about 120 bc), who was the 
2 CONT daughter of Ptolemy VI. In 140 bc, during a war with Parthia, Demetrius 
2 CONT was taken prisoner. In captivity he married Rodogune, daughter of the 
2 CONT Parthian king Mithradates I (reigned 171-138 bc). Demetrius II returned to 
2 CONT Syria in 129 bc. His brother, Antiochus VII (born 158? bc), who had 
2 CONT usurped the throne in 139 bc and married Cleopatra Thea, was killed in 
2 CONT battle in 127 bc. Demetrius II then regained the crown and reigned until 
2 CONT his assassination two years later. His son, Seleucus V, succeeded him but 
2 CONT enjoyed a short reign; he was put to death by his mother, Cleopatra Thea, 
2 CONT in the same year.
