0 @N6000000002187712549@ NOTE Antonia Minor
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Antonia Minor ("the younger") (30 January/31 January 36 BC - May-October 37 AD) was the daughter of Mark Antony and Octavia.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Drusus -- the son of the empress Livia and brother of the emperor Tiberius -- and their children were Germanicus, Livilla, and the emperor Claudius. She was the grandmother of Caligula and Agrippina the Younger, and th
1 CONC e great-grandmother of Nero.
1 CONT
1 CONT She never remarried after the death of her husband in 9 BC. When her son Germanicus died in 19 AD, she was forbidden to go to the funeral by orders of Tiberius and Livia. After the death of Livia in 29 AD, she took care of Germani
1 CONC cus and Agrippina the Elder's youngest children---Caligula and Drusilla.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 31 AD she exposed a plot between her daughter Livilla and Sejanus, Tiberius's Praetorian Prefect. This led to Sejanus's downfall and to the death of Livilla. Tiberius handed Livilla over to her formidable mother. According to T
1 CONC acitus, Antonia imprisoned Livilla in her rooms, effectively starving her to death. Claudius, her biggest disappointment (she once called him a "monster") was the only one of her children to survive her.
1 CONT
1 CONT She committed suicide in 37 AD on Caligula's orders after expressing unhappiness over the murder of her youngest grandson, Tiberius Gemellus. There is a passage in Suetonius's "Life of Gaius" that mentions how Caligula may have gi
1 CONC ven her poison himself.
