0 @N6000000002187718653@ NOTE Antiochus Theos
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus I Theos Dikaios Epiphanes Philorhomaios Philhellen (69–40 BC), was the most important king of the small Middle East kingdom of Commagene, which was situated in a region that is now in south-eastern Turkey and inhabited l
1 CONC argely by ethnic Kurds.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus was the son of king Mithradates I Callinicus and the Seleucid princess Laodice, daughter of the Syrian King Antiochus VIII. This marriage had been arranged as part of a settlement by Mithradates' father Samos II to ensur
1 CONC e peace between the Kingdom of Commagene and the Seleucid Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus tried to balance the interests of his kingdom with the reality of the world power of the Roman Empire and preserve as much as possible Commagene's independence. Despite his efforts, his reign ended with the Commagene sub
1 CONC mitting to Rome, becoming a client-state under EmperorAugustus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anthiochus was a king who took his religion, a Hellenized form of Zoroastrianism, very seriously. He left behind a large number of Greek inscriptions, revealing many aspects of the royal cult he promoted. The kings of Commagene cl
1 CONC aimed descent from both the Persian and Selucid royal houses, therefore the gods they worshipped were a syncretism of Greek and Persian deities, some of them were personifications of the Sun, Moon and planets. He is said to nhave 
1 CONC practised astrology of a very esoteric kind, and layed the basis for a calendrical reform, by linking the Commagene year, which till then had been based on the movements of the Moon, to the Sothic (Star of Sirius) cycle used by th
1 CONC e Egyptians as the basis of their calendar. This would suggest that Antiochus was knowledgeable about, if not fully initiated into the Hermetic tradition.
1 CONT
1 CONT Today Antiochus is most famous for founding the impressive sanctuary of Nemrud Dagi, which is close to the city of Kahta on a road leading to Mount Nimrod. Its most important monument is his so-called Hierothesion, where his remai
1 CONC ns were buried. It was constructed not to be a simple tomb but as a place where he would gather his people for religious festivities connected with either his birthday or coronation. Forgotten for centuries, it was excavated in 18
1 CONC 83 by a German team of archeologists.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kahta, the gateway to the lost kingdom of Commagene, is situated north of the modern Turkish civil engineering project Ataturk Baraji (Dam), which distributes the waters of the Euphrates River. Unfortunately, the lake it formed dr
1 CONC owned Commagene's original capital of Samosata, built by Samos I in the early 3rd century B.C.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Litterature
1 CONT Adrian G. Gilbert, Magi, Bloomsbury Publishing Plc., 1996
0 @N6000000002188286983@ NOTE Scribonia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Scribonia (68 BC-16) was the daughter of Lucius Scribonius Libo and Cornelia Sulla, the granddaughter of Pompey the Great and Lucius Cornelius Sulla. Her brother of the same name was consul and died in 34 BC.[1] She was the second
1 CONC  wife of Roman Emperor Augustus and the mother of his only natural child, Julia Caesaris. She was the grandmother of Gaius Caesar, Julia the Younger, Lucius Caesar, Agrippina the Elder and Agrippa Postumus, great-grandmother to Em
1 CONC peror Caligula and Empress Agrippina the Younger, who was the wife of the Emperor Claudius. She was the great-great-grandmother of Emperor Nero.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Life 
1 CONT 2 Marriages and issues 
1 CONT 3 Scribonia in popular culture 
1 CONT 3.1 Literature 
1 CONT 3.2 Drama 
1 CONT 4 Notes 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Little is known of Scribonia's early life. According to Suetonius, her first two marriages were to former consuls. Her first husband is unknown, though it had been suggested that he was Gnaeus Cornelius Lentulus Marcellinus (consu
1 CONC l 56 BC), as there is an inscription that refers to freedmen (post 39 BC) of Scribonia and her son Cornelius Marcellinus [2][3], indicating that she had a son from her previous marriage and that he was living with her after she di
1 CONC vorced her third husband. He may have died young and ignored by historians. Her second husband was Publius Cornelius Scipio Salvito[4]. They had a daughter Cornelia Scipio who married Lucius Aemilius Paullus who served as a censor
1 CONC . Scribonia may have also been the mother to Publius Cornelius Scipio, cousul in 16 BC. Salvito was a supporter of Pompey.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 40 BC Scribonia was forced to divorce her husband and marry Octavian, who was younger than her by several years. Octavian in turn divorced his wife Clodia, marrying Scribonia to cement a political alliance with her uncle Sextus
1 CONC  Pompeius. Their daughter Julia the Elder was born in 39 BC, probably in October, and on that very same day Octavian divorced her [5]. Their marriage had not been a happy one; Octavian felt she nagged him too much. She never remar
1 CONC ried. Cassius Dio and Marcus Velleius Paterculus says that when her youngest child, Julia, was sent into exile for adultery and treason, she requested that she be allowed to accompany her. [6]
1 CONT
1 CONT When Emperor Tiberius came into power, he separated Scribonia from her daughter, and allegedly starved Julia to death. When Scribonia died is unknown. It is mainly placed two years after Julia and Augustus. In Seneca, she is menti
1 CONC oned as being alive and in full possession of her wits as late as the end of 16 when she tried to convince her nephew Marcus Scribonius Libo not to commit suicide and face his punishment.
1 CONT
1 CONT No one knows what Scribonia was really like as her image as a shrew was likely to have been the end product of propaganda to divert the potentially scandalous circumstances of her divorce from Augustus. Seneca describes her as a g
1 CONC ravis femina; gravis meaning “dignified” and “severe”. Modern scholars are divided on her character; while some describe her as "tiresome" and "morose" [7] most others view her as an ideal example of a Roman matron as she clearly 
1 CONC had the "composure" and "calmness" to look after depressed and suicidal characters such as her daughter and nephew [8][9]. Sextus Propertius praises her motherhood referring to her as "sweet mother Scribonia" in Cornelia Scipio's 
1 CONC funeral elegy in 16 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and issues
1 CONT Her first husband, Gnaeus Cornelius Lentulus Marcellinus 
1 CONT Cornelius Marcellinus 
1 CONT Her second husband, Publius Cornelius Scipio Salvito 
1 CONT Publius Cornelius Scipio (consul of 16 BC) 
1 CONT Cornelia Scipio 
1 CONT Her third husband, Augustus 
1 CONT Julia the Elder (wife of Agrippa and Tiberius) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Scribonia in popular culture
1 CONT Little is known about Scribonia, but she is mentioned in various dramas and novels, each having a different opinion on what she was like.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Literature
1 CONT Scribonia is mentioned in Robert Graves's novel I, Claudius when he recalls Julia's birth and later when Julia is exiled. He describes her as a good, moderate and generally kind Roman matron. She is forbidden to see Julia and is o
1 CONC nly allowed to be with her once she is exiled. Livia convinces Augustus that Scribonia has been unfaithful to him causing him to divorce her faster than he cared to. Evidently Augustus believed she was innocent, as he kept Julia. 
1 CONC Graves places Scribonia's death at least two years prior to when it is traditionally placed. 
1 CONT Scribonia gets several mentions in the novel Augustus by Allan Massie. Allan Massie portrays her stereotypically, being ugly, gap-toothed and fat. The novel suggests that Julia got her personality from Scribonia rather than August
1 CONC us as historians tend to claim. 
1 CONT Scribonia plays a major role in the novel Caesar's Daughter by Edward Burton, trying to aid Julia in her daily life. She is a very politically aware woman, with detailed information gathering and she plays patroness to many poets 
1 CONC such as Horace and Ovid as well as being very popular with the people of Rome. Despite their differences, Augustus respects her. 
1 CONT Scribonia is mentioned in I Loved Tiberius by Elisabeth Dored. Augustus' reign is portrayed as a dictatorship and Scribonia is portrayed as a pretty, gentle, sensitive, warm and steadfast woman made a victim of her husband's cruel
1 CONC ty but eventually makes herself a martyr for her daughter, Julia. 
1 CONT She also is shown in Antony and Cleopatra by Colleen McCullough. Scribonia is described a beautiful, kind and sensible. She remains friends with Octavia following her divorce and, contrary to historians accounts, is allowed to rai
1 CONC se Julia herself. Augustus orders that Julia be educated in the manner of a man, rather than a woman. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Drama
1 CONT Scribonia in Imperium: Augustus is only a few years older than Augustus, and he marries her for her money to pay his armies. Maecenas describes her as being "lovely" and "charming". Julia is loyal to Scribonia blaming Augustus for
1 CONC  his bad treatment of her and using her just used her to get a baby. However, Augustus claims he loved Scribonia in his own way because she gave him Julia. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Schied, J. Scribonia Caesaris et les Julio-Claudiens: Problèmes de vocabulaire de parenté. Mémoires de l'École francaise de Rome et Athènes. 87: 349-71. 
1 CONT ^ CIL 6.26033: Libertorum et familiae Scribonae Caes. et Corneli Marcell. f. eius 
