0 @N6000000002187600307@ NOTE Tigranes the Great
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tigranes the Great (ruled 95 BC-56 BC) (also called Tigranes II and sometimes Tigranes I) was a king of Armenia. He was the son of Artavasdes I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 100 BC the Parthians placed him on the throne of Armenia, in return for "seventy valleys" in Armenia (Strabo 11.14.15). He rapidly built up his power, allying with Mithridates VI of Pontus and marrying his daughter Cleopatr
1 CONC a. Ultimately the two kings' attempts to control Cappadocia resulted in Roman intervention, in the person of Lucius Cornelius Sulla.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tigranes also expanded his domain into Parthia, going into Media as far as Ecbatana and to Arbela in Assyria, capturing northern Mesopotamia. In 83 BC he took over Syria, Phoenicia, and Cilicia, effectively putting an end to the S
1 CONC eleucid dynasty. Many of the inhabitants of conquered cities were sent to his new metropolis Tigranocerta.
1 CONT
1 CONT But his empire was not a lasting one. In 69 BC he warred with Rome, eventually losing Tigranocerta to Lucullus and being separated from Mithridates by Pompey in 66 BC. Tigranes' son went over to Pompey, and as they approached Arta
1 CONC xata, Tigranes himself surrendered, gave up all his territories except Armenia, and finished out his life as a tributary of Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was suceeded as king of Armenia by his son, Artavasdes II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Philip I and Antiochus XII Ruler of the Seleucid Empire Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antiochus XIII 
0 @N6000000002187712836@ NOTE Aurelia Cotta
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Aurelia Cotta or Aurelia (120BC-54BC) was a daughter to Rutilia and Lucius Aurelius Cotta. Her father was consul in 119BC and her paternal grandfather of the same name was consul in 144BC. The Aurelii Cottae family were prominent 
1 CONC during the Roman Republican era. Her mother Rutilia, was a member of the Rutilius family. They were of consular rank.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her brother a younger Lucius Aurelius Cotta was consul in 74BC. Gaius Aurelius Cotta consul in 75BC and his brother Marcus Aurelius Cotta consul in 65BC, were her first cousins and were sons to her paternal uncle Gaius Aurelius Co
1 CONC tta.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aurelia married a praetor, Gaius Julius Caesar III. Her husband died 85BC-84BC. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Julia Caesaris the Elder (died young) 
1 CONT Julia Caesaris the Younger 101BC-51BC 
1 CONT Gaius Julius Caesar IV ‘The Dictator’ 100BC-44BC 
1 CONT The Historian Tacitus, considers her as an ideal Roman Matron and thinks highly of her. Plutarch describes her as a ‘strict and respectable’ woman.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aurelia and her family were very influential in her son’s upbringing and security. She was involved in petitions to save her son and her cousin, the consul Gaius Aurelius Cotta, defended young Caesar to the Dictator Lucius Corneli
1 CONC us Sulla.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the Bona Dea Festival, held at Caesar’s house, during sacred ceremonies she caught her daughter-in-law Pompeia Sulla having an affair with Publius Clodius Pulcher. Due to this Caesar, divorced her.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: Suetonius - The Twelve Caesars - Caesar, Plutarch - Fall of the Roman Republic - Caesar, www.ancientworlds.net/aw/Post/186447 and List of Roman Republican Consuls from Wikipedia.
