0 @N6000000002187599912@ NOTE Poppilia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT The Popilii Laenates was the name of an ancient Roman Plebeian family, who lived during the Roman Republic Era. The Popilii were known for their cruel and arrogant nature. The gens name Popilii is of Etruscan origin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Poppilia or Poppillia was the name given to the women of the Popilius Laenas family. During the Roman Republic, there were two women with the name Poppilia:
1 CONT
1 CONT Poppilia, wife of Lucius Julius Caesar II. Their children were Lucius Julius Caesar III (consul of 90 BC and later senator) and a Gaius Julius Caesar Strabo Vopiscus (a humble orator). Strabo Vopiscus might have had a twin brother
1 CONC . She was the paternal grandmother to the consul Lucius Julius Caesar IV and Julia Antonia (mother to Roman Triumvir Mark Antony). Poppilia died in December 110 BC. 
1 CONT Poppilia, wife of consul and Roman statesman Quintus Lutatius Catulus. They had a younger Quintus Lutatius Catulus (120 BC - 61 BC) who would become the consul in 78 BC, become an ally to Roman General Pompey the Great and would d
1 CONC islike Julius Caesar. This Poppilia died, after her husband in 87 BC. According to Cicero (de. Orat. ii. 11.) she was the first Roman woman to received a funeral eulogy over her grave. The eulogy was given by her son and this was 
1 CONC at the time, the highest honor given to a woman in Rome. 
1 CONT Sources:
1 CONT
1 CONT Microsoft Encarta Encyclopaedia 2002 - Quintus Lutatius Catulus 
0 @N6000000002187624463@ NOTE Cleopatra Thea
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra Thea (c. 164 BC - 121 BC) surnamed Euergetis (i.e., "Benefactress") was a daughter of Ptolemy VI of Egypt and Cleopatra II of Egypt. She ruled Seleucid Syria from 125 BC to 121 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married Alexander Balas in about 150 BC. This union produced Antiochus VI Dionysus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her second marriage was to Demetrius II Nicator in about 148 BC. Their children were Seleucus V Philometor, Antiochus VIII Grypus, and possibly a daughter (Laodice?). Demetrius was captured fighting against the Parthians and was c
1 CONC onsequently off the scene for a while.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her third husband, married during Demetrius' captivity, was his brother Antiochus VII Sidetes. They certainly had Antiochus IX Cyzicenus, but the names of any other children are uncertain. In about 129 BC, Antiochus was killed fig
1 CONC hting the Parthians. Demetrius had been released and returned home to reclaim his throne and queen. Cleopatra had taken the precaution of sending Antiochus IX (her son by Antiochus VII) to Cyzicus in Asia Minor (hence his surname)
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Demetrius unsuccessfully attempted to invade Egypt. In retaliation, the ruler of Egypt put forward Alexander Zabinas, allegedly a son of Alexander Balas as king of Syria, provoking civil war. After a defeat by Zabinas' forces at D
1 CONC amascus, Demetrius retreated to Ptolemais Hermiou only to find the city gates closed against him by Cleopatra. He boarded a ship to flee, but was killed on Cleopatra's orders.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 125 BC to 121 BC, Cleopatra ruled Syria, killing Demetrius' eldest son Seleucus when he attempted to claim the throne. To legitimize her reign, she shared the throne with her son Antiochus VIII Grypus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Antiochus became less controllable as he grew up and in 121 BC, she decided to eliminate him. As he returned from a hunt one day, she offered him a cup of wine. Since this was not common behavior for her, Antiochus was suspicious 
1 CONC and forced her to drink the wine, with the predictable outcome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alexander II Seleucid Ruler Succeeded by:
1 CONT Antiochus VIII 
0 @N6000000002187624086@ NOTE Cleopatra III of Egypt
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Cleopatra III (Greek: ??e?p?t?a, 161–101 BC) was queen of Egypt 142–101 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was born in 161 BC to Ptolemy VI and Cleopatra II of Egypt. Ptolemy VII Neos Philopater was her brother. After the death of her father, her brother became the King of Egypt, and her mother was regent. In an attempt to gain con
1 CONC trol of Cleopatra II, Ptolemy VIII Euergetes II "Physcon" married her. Physcon later had Philopater killed, thus taking the throne for himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT He then discarded Cleopatra II and married Cleopatra III in 142 BC, making her Queen and joint ruler. His reign after that was peaceful, but he was very despotic. She bore Physcon these children: Ptolemy IX Philometor Soter II Lat
1 CONC hyros, Ptolemy X Alexander I, Cleopatra IV, Cleopatra V Tryphaena, and Cleopatra Selene.
1 CONT
1 CONT When he died, he left the throne to Cleopatra and whichever son she wished. If it were up to her, she would have chosen her younger son Alexander, but the Alexandrians forced her to bring Lathyros from Cyprus, of which he was gove
1 CONC rnor, to co-rule, becoming Ptolemy IX. The younger son Alexander was then sent to Cyprus to fill Lathyros's former position.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually, by accusing Lathyros of murdering her, she successfully deposed him and brought Alexander back to Egypt, becoming Ptolemy X. However, she eventually tired of Alexander and brought back Lathyros. Again, she grew tired o
1 CONC f Lathyros as well, and Alexander was brought back. Tiring of his mother's intrigue, Alexander had her assassinated in 101 BC.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra II Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT First Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VIII and Cleopatra II Succeeded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra II
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Cleopatra II Ptolemaic Queen of Egypt
1 CONT Second Reign
1 CONT with Ptolemy VIII, Cleopatra II, Ptolemy IX, and Ptolemy X Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy X
0 @N6000000001353854685@ NOTE Event: Ruled 170 - 164 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt [Alexandria] [co-regent with Ptolemy VI] 1
1 CONT Event: Ruled 145 - 116 BC, Pharaoh of Egypt 2
1 CONT Event: Ruled 163 - 145 BC, King of Cyrenaica [Libya] 1
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII EUERGETES II (Greek: "Benefactor II"), also called PHYSCON (Greek: "Potbellied") (d. 116 BC), Macedonian king of Egypt who played a divisive role in trying to win the kingship, making himself subservient to Rome and e
1 CONC ncouraging Roman interference in Egypt.
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII ruled jointly with his brother, Ptolemy VI Philometor, in 170-164 BC and alone during the next year; he was king of Cyrenaica(in modern Libya) in 163-145, and sole ruler of Egypt from 145 to hisdeath in 116, except fo
1 CONC r a brief exile in 131-129. Continuously quarreling with his queen, Cleopatra II, the widow of Philometor, he causedcivil war and economic collapse in Egypt. Late in his reign (118) he instituted extensive reforms to restore the c
1 CONC ountry.
1 CONT Around 117 an expedition sponsored by Ptolemy completed the first seavoyage to India via the Red Sea and Indian Ocean, beginning Egypt's interest in the spice trade. [Encyclopædia Britannica CD '97, PTOLEMY VIII EUERGETES II]
1 CONT ----------
1 CONT Ptolemy VIII Euergetes II, repulsive and nicknamed 'physon' [potbelly], was captivated by his niece, also Cleopatra, the daughter of his sister-wife Cleopatra. The niece agreed to the liaison so long as she could also become queen
1 CONC  -- so mother and daughter, sister and niece of Euergetes, became joint queens as Cleopatra II and III...The former wasmuch beloved by the people since her late husband Ptolemy VI's reign was such a shining example and memory comp
1 CONC ared to their present situation. Public resentment against Ptolemy VIII grew to such a point that he fled to Cyprus, taking the younger Cleopatra (III), their two children and the young boy Memphites (his son by Cleopatra II) with
1 CONC  him. His flight was not a moment too soon, for the mob broke into the palaceseeking his blood.
1 CONT In Cyprus, Euergetes plotted his return to Egypt where his sister, Cleopatra II, reigned as Cleopatra Philometor Soteira. In a fit of maniacal revenge against his sister and the Alexandrian mob which had been busy destroying his s
1 CONC tatues and memories of him, he murdered Memphites, his own son by Cleopatra II, and sent the child's dismembered body to her as a present on her birthday.
1 CONT In 129, now strong enough to invade Egypt, Euergetes returned from Cyprus and in 128 Cleopatra II fled for protection to her daughter, Cleopatra Thea, now married to Demetrius II of Syria. Strangely, she was to return to Egypt, an
1 CONC d Euergetes survived until 116. What happened tohis sister-wife Cleopatra II after her return is not known, but she presumably predeceased him as her daughter, Cleopatra III, inherited Egypt by Euergetes' will.
