1 CONC rtly after his accession and marriage, Ptolemy invaded Coele Syria, to avenge the murder of his sister, the widow of the Seleucid king Antiochus II. Ptolemy's navy, perhaps aided by rebels in the cities, advanced against Seleucus 
1 CONC II's forces as far as Thrace, across the Hellespont, and also captured some islands off the AsiaMinor coast, but were checked c. 245. Meanwhile, Ptolemy, with the army, penetrated deep into Mesopotamia, reaching at least Seleucia 
1 CONC on the Tigris, near Babylon. According to classical sources he was compelled to halt his advance because of domestic troubles. Famine and a low Nile, as well as the hostile alliance between Macedonia, Seleucid Syria, and Rhodes, w
1 CONC ere perhaps additional reasons. The war in Asia Minor and the Aegean intensified as the Achaean League, one of the Greek confederations, allied itself to Egypt, while Seleucus II secured two allies in the Black Sea region. Ptolemy
1 CONC  was pushed out of Mesopotamia andpart of North Syria in 242-241, and the next year peace was finally achieved. Ptolemy managed to keep the Orontes River region and Antioch,both in Syria; Ephesus, in Asia Minor; and Thrace and per
1 CONC haps also Cilicia.
1 CONT Within Egypt, Ptolemy continued the colonization of al-Fayyum (the oasis-like depression southwest of Cairo), which his father had developed. He also reformed the calendar, adopting 311 as the first year of a "Ptolemaic Era." The 
1 CONC Canopus decree, a declaration published by a synod of Egyptian priests, suggests that the true duration of the year (365 1/4 days) was now recognized, for an extra day was added to the calendar every four years. The new calendar f
1 CONC ailed, however, to achieve popular acceptance. The priests and classical sources also credited Ptolemy with the restoration of the divine statues plundered from the temples during Persian rule. In addition, the King initiated cons
1 CONC tructionat Edfu, the Upper Egyptian site of a great Ptolemaic temple, and made donations to other temples.
1 CONT Ptolemy avoided involvement in the wars that continued to plague Syria and Macedonia. He did, however, send aid to Rhodes, after earthquakes devastated the island, but he refrained from subsidizing the schemes of the Spartan king 
1 CONC against Macedonia, though he granted him asylumin 222. In Asia Minor, when a pretender to one of the kingdoms, who was the instigator of much of the trouble there, sought asylum in Ptolemaic territory, Ptolemy promptly interned hi
1 CONC m. His policy was to maintain an equilibrium of power, guaranteeing the safety of his own territory. After declaring his son his successor, Ptolemy died, leaving Egypt at the peak of its political power, and internally stable and 
1 CONC prosperous
0 @N6000000002187624165@ NOTE Ptolemy III Euergetes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy III Euergetes, (Greek:?t??eµa??? ??e???t??, reigned 246 BC–222 BC) is sometimes called Ptolemy III Euergetes I. The third ruler of the Ptolemaic dynasty in Egypt, he was the eldest son of Ptolemy II Philadelphus and his fi
1 CONC rst wife Arsinoe I. He came to power in 246 BC upon the death of his father. He is most noted for his invasions of the northern kingdom of Syria which he commenced upon the murder of his eldest sister Berenice Phernophorus.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Berenice of Cyrene in the year corresponding to 244/243 BC; and they were parents of Arsinoe III and Ptolemy IV Philopator.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy III Euergetes had put up the first of the Rosetta Stone series, the bilingual inscriptions on massive stone blocks in three writing systems. Ptolemy III's stone stela is the Canopus Stone of 238 B.C.E. His son, Ptolemy IV,
1 CONC  is responsible for the Memphis Stele, or Memphis Stone, the second in the series, bearing the Decree of Memphis, about 218 B.C.E. The famous Rosetta Stone is the third, erected by Ptolemy V, in 196 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ptolemy III's stone contains decrees about priestly orders, and is a memorial for his daughter Berenice. But two of its 26 lines of hieroglyphs decree the use of a leap day added to the Egyptian calendar of 365 days, and the assoc
1 CONC iated changes in festivals.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ptolemaic Egypt- Egyptian history during the Ptolemaic dynasty.
1 CONT     * Ptolemais - towns and cities named after members of the Ptolemaic dynasty.
1 CONT     * Leap year.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ptolemy Euergetes I at LacusCurtius — (Chapter VI of E. R Bevan's House of Ptolemy, 1923)
1 CONT     * Ptolemy III — (Royal Egyptian Genealogy)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy II Philadelphus Lagidae Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ptolemy IV Philopator
0 @N6000000001353854631@ NOTE Berenice II (b. c. 269--d. 221 BC), daughter of Magas, king of Cyrene(in modern Libya), whose marriage to Ptolemy III Euergetes reunited her country with Egypt.
1 CONT Magas' queen, who favoured an alliance with the Seleucid dynasty of Syria, tried to thwart the marriage by summoning Demetrius the Fair, a Macedonian prince, as a husband for Berenice. The princess, however, arranged Demetrius' mu
1 CONC rder and married Ptolemy about 245. When Ptolemy set forth to avenge the murder of his sister (the widow of Antiochus II) in Syria, Berenice dedicated a lock of her hair for his safe return. According to the court astronomer, it w
1 CONC as transferred to heaven, where it formed a new constellation that was consequently named Coma Berenices, "Hair of Berenice." Berenice and Ptolemy had four children: the future Ptolemy IV Philopator, Arsinoe III, Magas, and Bereni
1 CONC ce, whodied as a child. The queen survived her husband, but her son Ptolemy IV linked her to a plot with her father, Magas, and had her poisoned.
