0 @N6000000002187617622@ NOTE Mithridates I of Pontus
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates I Ctistes (in Greek M????d?t?? ?' Kt?st??; reigned 302 BC - 266 BC) was the founder (this is the meaning of the word Ctistes) of the kingdom of Pontus in Anatolia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 302 or 301 BC, shortly after having executed his father Mithridates of Cius, the diadoch Antigonus became suspicious of the son who had inherited the family dominion of Cius. Antigonus was motivated to form a plan oriented on k
1 CONC illing the boy. Mithridates Ktistes, however, received from Demetrius Poliorcetes timely notice of his father's intentions, and fled with a few followers to Paphlagonia, where he occupied a strong fortress, called Cimiata. He was 
1 CONC joined by numerous bodies of troops from different quarters and gradually extended his dominions in Pontus and created the foundations for the birth of a new kingdom, which may be judged to have risen about 281 BC when Mithridates
1 CONC  assumed the title of basileus (king).1 In the same year, we find him concluding an alliance with the town of Heraclea Pontica, in Bithynia, to protect them against Seleucus2. At a subsequent period, Mithridates is found acquiring
1 CONC  support from the Gauls (who later settled in Asia Minor) in order to overthrow a force sent against him by Ptolemy, king of Egypt.3 These are the recorded events of his reign, which lasted for thirty-six years.4 He was succeeded 
1 CONC by his son Ariobarzanes. He seems to have been buried in a royal grave near the kingdom's capital, Amasia. Next to him will be buried all the kings of Pontus until the fall of Sinope in 183 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Appian,5 he was eighth in descent from the first satrap of Pontus under Darius the Great and sixth in ascending order from Mithridates Eupator. However, this point is controversial since Plutarch6 writes that eight ge
1 CONC nerations of kings of Pontus stemmed from him before Roman subjection.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Mithridates II of Cius King of Pontus
1 CONT 302 BC – 266 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ariobarzanes
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Appian, The foreign wars, Horace White (translator), New York, (1899)
1 CONT     * Hazel, John; Who's Who in the Greek World, "Mithridates I" (1999)
1 CONT     * Memnon, History of Heracleia, Andrew Smith (translator), (2004)
1 CONT     * Plutarch, Parallel Lives, "Demetrius", John & William Langhorne (translator), (1770)
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Mithridates III", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT     * Strabo, Geography, H. C. Hamilton & W. Falconer (translators), London, (1903)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Appian, The Foreign Wars, "The Mithridatic Wars", 9; Strabo, Geography, xii. 3; Plutarch, Lives, "Demetrius", 4
1 CONT 2 Memnon, History of Heraclea, 7
1 CONT 3 Stephanus, Ethnica, s. v. Ancyra
1 CONT 4 Diodorus Siculus, Histoire Universelle, xx. 111, pag. 457
1 CONT 5 Appian, 112
1 CONT 6 Plutarch, 4
1 CONT
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the public domain Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology by William Smith (1867).
0 @N6000000002187617695@ NOTE Mithridates I of Cius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates (in Greek M????d?t??; lived 4th century BC), son of Ariobarzanes prince of Cius, is mentioned by Xenophon1 as having betrayed his father, and the same circumstance is alluded to by Aristotle2. It is possible that he is
1 CONC  the same Mithridates who accom­panied the younger Cyrus, but there is no proof of this; also, he may be the same with the Mithridates mentioned by Xenophon3 as satrap of Cappadocia and Lycaonia. It appears that he was dead before
1 CONC  363 BC, when Ariobarzanes II made himself master of the family fiefdom of Cius in Mysia.4
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Aristotle, Politics, H. Rackham (translator), Cambridge, MA - London, (1944)
1 CONT     * Diodorus Siculus, Bibliotheca, C. H. Oldfather (translator), Cambridge, MA - London, (1989)
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Mithridates I", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT     * Xenophon, Cyropaedia, Cambridge, MA - London, (1979-83)
1 CONT     * Xenophon, Anabasis, Cambridge, MA - London, (1980)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Xenophon, Cyropaedia, viii. 8. 4
1 CONT 2 Aristotle, v. 10
1 CONT 3 Xenophon, Anabasis, vii. 8. 25
1 CONT 4 Diodorus, xv. 90
0 @N6000000002187622743@ NOTE Mithridates II of Cius
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mithridates of Cius (in Greek M????d?t?? or M???ad?t??; lived c. 386–302 BC, ruled 337–302 BC) succeeded his father Ariobarzanes II in 337 BC as ruler of the Greek town of Cius in Mysia (today part of Turkey).
1 CONT
1 CONT Diodorus assigns him a rule of thirty-five years, but it appears that he did not hold uninterrupted possession of the sovereignty during that period1. What circumstances led to his expulsion or subjection we know not; nothing is h
1 CONC eard of him till his death in 302, but it appears that he had submitted to the Macedonian Antigonus, who now, to prevent him from joining the league of Cassander and his confederates, procured his assassination in Cius.2 According
1 CONC  to Lucian3, he was not less than eighty-four years of age at the time of his death, which renders it not improbable that he is the same as the Mithridates, son of Ariobarzanes, who in his youth circumvented and put to death Datam
1 CONC es.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Appian, The foreign wars, Horace White (translator), New York, (1899)
1 CONT     * Diodorus Siculus, Bibliotheca, C. H. Oldfather (translator), Cambridge - London, (1989)
1 CONT     * Højte, Jacob Munte; The Date of the Alliance between Chersonesos and Pharnakes and its implications
1 CONT     * Smith, William (editor); Dictionary of Greek and Roman Biography and Mythology, "Mithridates II", Boston, (1867)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes
1 CONT 1 Diodorus, xvi. 90
1 CONT 2 Appian, "Mithridatic Wars", 9; Diodorus, xx. 111, pag. 456
1 CONT 3 Lucian, Macrobioi, 13
