0 @N6000000002187614225@ NOTE Achaemenes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenes (Hakaimanish) was the eponymous ancestor of the royal house of the first Persian Empire, the Achaemenid dynasty. He lived about 2,700 years ago. His name comes from the Old Persian Hakhamanish, meaning "friendly in natu
1 CONC re." The name's Hellenized form is Achaimenes, which has become rendered in English as Achaemenes. Achaemenes did not rule all of Iran, but a small place in the northwestern part of the country near Lake Urmia, which Assyrian insc
1 CONC riptions call Parsumash, or land of the Parsu or Persians.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenes' royal descendants revered him and credited him as the founder of their dynasty, but very little was ever remembered about his specific achievements. Most likely he was a warrior chieftain who led the Persians, or a tri
1 CONC be of Persians, as a vassal of the Median Empire. An Assyrian inscription from the time of King Sennacherib mentions that the Assyrian king repelled a raid by the Parsu, who may have been led by Achaemenes. Ancient Greek writers p
1 CONC rovide some other legendary information: they call his tribe the Pasargadae and say that he was raised by an eagle. Plato, when writing about the Persians, oddly speculated that Achaemenes was the son of the Greek hero Perseus and
1 CONC  a grandson of Zeus. He might have confused Achaemenes with Perses, son of Perseus by Andromeda and ancestor of the Persians in Greek mythology. Perses was probably a legendary ancestor of Achaemenes himself from the viewpoint of 
1 CONC later writers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenes was succeeded by his son Teispes, who would lead the Persians to conquer and settle in the city of Anshan. His great-grandson was Cyrus II, who conquered the Medes and established the Persian Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Due to the lack of hard information on Achaemenes, it has been contended that epigraphic evidence for his existence and rule is highly suspect; in fact, he may have been a Persian legend or an invention of Darius the Great.
0 @N6000000002187614761@ NOTE Shamash-shum-ukin
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shamash-shum-ukin was king of Babylon from 669-648 BC. He was the second son of the Assyrian King Esarhaddon. His elder brother, crown prince Sin-iddina-apla had died in 672, and in his stead the third son Assurbanipal was investe
1 CONC d as crown prince and later king of Assyria, while Shamash-shum-ukin remained crown prince of Babylonia. Provincial governors and vassals had to take an oath to accept this and to help the brothers gain their respective thrones in
1 CONC  the event of Esarhaddon's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Esarhaddon unexpectedly died on a campaign against rebellious Egypt in 669, it was only the decisive action of Naqi'a-Zakutu, widow of Sennacherib, that got Assurbanipal on the throne, in the face of opposition by court offic
1 CONC ials and parts of the priesthood. Shamash-shum-ukin, the older brother, became viceroy of Babylonia. The arrangement was evidently intended to flatter the Babylonians by giving them once more the semblance of independence. But it 
1 CONC failed to work, as Shamash-shum-ukin became more Babylonian than his subjects, and claimed to be the successor of the monarchs whose empire had once stretched to the Mediterranean.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Babylonian territory consisted of Babylon, Borsippa, Kutha and Sippar. While Shamash-shum-ukin was sovereign ruler of the south in theory, Assyria maintained a garrison in Nippur, and some of the provincial governors tried to 
1 CONC get into Assyrian favour. Letters by Sin-balassu-iqbi, governor of Ur show how he tried to ingratiate himself with Assurbanipal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Assurbanipal took an active part in the restoration of sanctuaries in the south. A stela now in London commemorates his help in restoring E-sagila (the tower of Babel), another tells of how he restored the Nabu-temple in Borsippa.
1 CONC  Even the Sumerian language was revived as the official tongue.
1 CONT
1 CONT In May 652, Shamash-shum-ukin rose in rebellion. The reasons for this are completely unknown, as no Babylonian sources have been preserved. He was joined by Nabu-bel-shumate, king of the sea-country, the Elamites, the Chaldean tri
1 CONC bes of the South, the kings of Guti, Amurru and Melukka and tribes from the Arab desert. After two years Babylon and Borsippa were besieged, and Elam was beaten. Babylon yielded in June 648 because of starvation. The sources descr
1 CONC ibe cases of cannibalism. Shamash-shum-ukin threw himself into his burning palace, to be remembered by the Greeks in the story of Sardanapal. His successor on the throne of Babylon was Kandalanu (647-627). Kandalanu, however, may 
1 CONC have been a throne name taken by Assurbanipal himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT ABC 15: Chronicle Concerning the Reign of Šamaš-šuma-ukin 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Esarhaddon King of Babylon
1 CONT 668–648 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Kandalanu 
0 @N6000000002187613900@ NOTE Phraortes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Fravartish or Phraortes (c. 665 - 633 BC), son of Deioces, was the second king of the Media and the founder of Median government.
1 CONT
1 CONT Like his father, Fravartish started wars against Assyria, but was defeated and killed by Ashurbanipal, the king of Assyria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Phraortes was followed by his son Cyaxares. He led his force to Lydia but was stopped in front of the river Halys due a sudden eclipse. Then he followed in the footsteps of his father and marched on his way to Assyria, but this ti
1 CONC me victory was on the side of the medians.
1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Median Empire
1 CONT
1 CONT The Median Empire, was the first Iranian dynasty corresponding to the northeastern section of present-day Iran, Northern-Khvarvarana and Asuristan (now days known as Iraq), and South and Eastern Anatolia. The inhabitants, who were
1 CONC  known as Medes, and their neighbors, the Persians, spoke Median languages that were closely related to Aryan (Old Persian). Historians know very little about the Iranian culture under the Median dynasty, except that Zoroastrianis
1 CONC m as well as a polytheistic religion was practiced, and a priestly caste called the Magi existed. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Traditionally, the creator of the Median kingdom was one Deioces, who, according to Herodotus, reigned from 728 to 675 BCE and founded the Median capital Ecbatana (Hâgmatâna or modern Hamadan). Attempts have been made to associate
1 CONC  Daiaukku, a local Zagros king mentioned in a cuneiform text as one of the captives deported to Assyria by Sargon II in 714 BCE, with the Deioces of Herodotus, but such an association is highly unlikely. To judge from the Assyrian
1 CONC  sources, no Median kingdom such as Herodotus describes for the reign of Deioces existed in the early 7th century BCE; at best, he is reporting a Median legend of the founding of their kingdom. 
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Herodotus (History of Herodotus), Deioces was succeeded by his son Phraortes (675-653 BCE), who subjugated the Persians and lost his life in a premature attack against the Assyrians. Some of this tale may be true. Ass
1 CONC yrian texts speak of a Kashtariti as the leader of a conglomerate group of Medes, Scythians, Mannaeans, and miscellaneous other local Zagros peoples that seriously threatened the peace of Assyria's eastern borderlands during the r
1 CONC eign of Esarhaddon (680-669 BCE). It is possible that Phraortes is this Kashtariti, though the suggestion cannot be proved either historically or linguistically. That a Median king in this period exerted political and military con
1 CONC trol over the Persians is entirely reasonable, though it cannot be proved. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The lion of Ecabtana, Hephaestion's tomb 
1 CONT Beginning as early as the 9th century, and with increasing impact in the late 8th and early 7th centuries, groups of nomadic warriors entered western Iran, probably from across the Caucasus. Dominant among these groups were the Sc
1 CONC ythians, and their entrance into the affairs of the western plateau during the 7th century may perhaps mark one of the important turning points in Iron Age history. Herodotus speaks in some detail of a period of Scythian dominatio
1 CONC n, the so-called Scythian interregnum in Median dynasty history. His dating of this event remains uncertain, but traditionally it is seen as falling between the reigns of Phraortes and Cyaxares and as covering the years 653 to 625
1 CONC  BCE. Whether such an interregnum ever actually occurred and, if it did, whether it should not be dated later than this are open questions. What is clear is that, by the mid-7th century BCE, there were a great many Scythians in we
1 CONC stern Iran, that they, along with the Medes and other groups, posed a serious threat to Assyria, and that their appearance threw previous power alignments quite out of balance. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Herodotus reports how, under Cyaxares of Media (625-585 BCE), the Scythians were overthrown when their kings were induced at a supper party to get so drunk that they were then easily slain. It is more likely that about this time e
1 CONC ither the Scythians withdrew voluntarily from western Iran and went off to plunder elsewhere or they were simply absorbed into a rapidly developing confederation under Median hegemony. Cyaxares is a fully historical figure who app
1 CONC ears in the cuneiform sources as Uvakhshatra. Herodotus speaks of how Cyaxares reorganized the Median army into units built around specialized armaments: spearmen, bowmen, and cavalry. The unified and reorganized Medes were a matc
1 CONC h for the Assyrians. They attacked one of the important Assyrian border cities, Arrapkha, in 615 BCE, surrounded Nineveh in 614 BCE but were unable to capture it, and instead successfully stormed the Assyrian religious capital, As
1 CONC hur. An alliance between Babylon and the Medes was sealed by the betrothal of Cyaxares' granddaughter to Nabopolassar's son, Nebuchadrezzar II (605-562 BCE). In 612 BCE the attack on Nineveh was renewed, and the city fell in late 
1 CONC August (the Babylonians arrived rather too late to participate fully in the battle). The Babylonians and the Medes together pursued the fleeing Assyrians westward into Syria. Assyrian appeals to Egypt for help came to nought, and 
1 CONC the last Assyrian ruler, Ashur-uballit II, disappeared from history in 609 BCE. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Golden rhyton from Ecbatana
1 CONT Tehran (National Museum) 
1 CONT The problem, of course, was how to divide the spoils among the victors. The cuneiform sources are comparatively silent, but it would seem that the Babylonians fell heir to all of the Assyrian holdings within the fertile crescent, 
1 CONC while their allies took over all of the highland areas. The Medes gained control over the lands in eastern Anatolia that had once been part of Urartu and eventually became embroiled in war with the Lydians, the dominant political 
1 CONC power in western Asia Minor. In 585 BCE, probably through the mediation of the Babylonians, peace was established between Media and Lydia, and the Halys (Kizil) River was fixed as the boundary between the two kingdoms. Thus a new 
1 CONC balance of power was established in the Middle East among Medes, Lydians, Babylonians, and, far to the south, Egyptians. At his death, Cyaxares controlled vast territories: all of Anatolia to the Halys, the whole of western Iran e
1 CONC astward, perhaps as far as the area of modern Tehran, and all of south-western Iran, including Fars. Whether it is appropriate to call these holdings a kingdom is debatable; one suspects that authority over the various peoples, Ir
1 CONC anian and non-Iranian, who occupied these territories was exerted in the form of a confederation such as is implied by the ancient Iranian royal title, king of kings. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Astyages followed his father, Cyaxares, on the Median throne (585-550 BCE). Comparatively little is known of his reign. All was not well with the alliance with Babylon, and there is some evidence to suggest that Babylonia may have
1 CONC  feared Median power. The latter, however, was soon in no position to threaten others, for Astyages was himself under attack. Indeed, Astyages and the Medians were soon overthrown by the rise to power in the Iranian world of Cyrus
1 CONC  II the Great. 
0 @N6000000002187614016@ NOTE Sadyattes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sadyattes, son of Ardys II, was King of Lydia from 624 BC to 610 BC. He was succeeded by his son Alyattes II. The Greeks nicknamed him Candaules, 'the dog-strangler', after a name of the Lydian god Hermes. Herodotus wrote (in Inqu
1 CONC iries) that he fought with Cyaxares, the descendant of Deioces, and with the Medes, drove out the Cimmerians from Asia, took Smyrna, which had been founded by colonists from Colophon, and invaded Clazomenae and Miletus. After ruli
1 CONC ng for twelve years he was assassinated by his former friend Gyges, who succeeded him on the throne of Lydia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sadyattes"
1 CONT Categories: 610 BC deaths | Kings of Lydia
0 @N6000000002187614739@ NOTE Esarhaddon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Esarhaddon (Greek and Biblical form; Akkadian Aššur-aha-iddina "Ashur has given a brother to me"), was a king of Assyria who reigned 681 BC-669 BC), the youngest son of Sennacherib and the Aramaic queen Naqi'a (Zakitu), Sennacheri
1 CONC b's second wife. When, despite being the youngest son, he was named successor by his father, his elder brothers tried to discredit him. Oracles had named Esarhaddon as the person to free the exiles and rebuild Babylon, the destruc
1 CONC tion of which by Sennacherib was felt to be sacrilegious. Esarhaddon remained crown prince, but was forced into exile at an unknown place beyond Hanilgalbat (Mitanni), that is, beyond the Euphrates, most likely somewhere in what i
1 CONC s now southeastern Turkey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sennacherib was murdered in 681 BC, some claim at the instigation of Esarhaddon, though this seems hardly likely, as he was not in a situation to exploit unrest arising from the death of his father. He returned to the capital of N
1 CONC ineveh in forced marches and defeated his rival brothers in six weeks of civil war. He was formally declared king in spring of 681 BC. His brothers fled the land, and their followers and families were put to death. In the same yea
1 CONC r he began the rebuilding of Babylon, including the well-known Esagila (sometimes identified with Tower of Babel). The statues of the Babylonian gods were restored and returned to the city. In order not to appear too biased in fav
1 CONC or of Babylonia, he ordered the reconstruction of the Assyrian sanctuary of Esharra in Ashur as well. Foreigners were forbidden to enter this temple. Both buildings were dedicated almost at the same date, in year two of his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first military campaigns of Esarhaddon were directed against nomadic tribes of southern Mesopotamia, the Dakkuri and Gambulu, who had been harassing the peasants. In 679 BC the Cimmerians, who had already killed his grandfathe
1 CONC r Sargon, reappeared in Cilicia and Tabal under their new ruler Teushpa. Esarhaddon defeated them near Hubushna, and defeated the rebellious inhabitants of Hilakku as well. The Cimmerians withdrew to the west, where they were to d
1 CONC estroy the kingdom of Phrygia in 676, together with Scythian and Urartian help.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Sidonian king Abdi-Milkutti, who had risen up against the Assyrian king, was defeated in 677 BC and beheaded. The town of Sidon was destroyed and rebuilt as Kar-Ashur-aha-iddina, the Harbor of Esarhaddon. The population was de
1 CONC ported to Assyria. A share of the plunder went to the loyal king of rival Tyre. The partly conserved text of a treaty with Tyre mentions the kings of Judah, Edom, Moab, Gaza, Ashkelon, Ekron, Byblos, Arvad, Samsi-muruna, Ammon, As
1 CONC hdod, ten kings from the coast of the sea, and ten kings from the middle of the sea (usually identified with Cyprus), as Assyrian allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 676 BC Esarhaddon took the towns of Sissu and Kundu in the Taurus mountains. The Mannaeans, the Scythians under their king Ishpakaia, and the Guti of the Zagros proved to be a nuisance as well, as is attested by numerous oracle
1 CONC -texts. The Mannaeans, former vassals of the Assyrians, were no longer restricted to the area around Lake Urmia, but had spread into Zamua, where they interrupted the horse trade between Parsuash and Assyria and refused to pay fur
1 CONC ther tribute. After the fall of Phrygia, a daughter of Esarhaddon was wedded to the Scythian prince Partatua of Sakasene in order to improve relations with the nomads. The Medes under [Khshathrita (Phraortes) had been the target o
1 CONC f a campaign as well, the date of which is unclear (possibly before 676 BC). Later, Assyrian hosts reached the border of the "salt-desert" near the mountain Bikni, that is, near Teheran. A number of fortresses secured the Zagros: 
1 CONC Bit-Parnakki, Bit-kari and Harhar (Kar-Sharrukin).
1 CONT
1 CONT A certain Mugallu had taken possession of parts of Meliddu (Melitene), and associated himself with the king of Tabal. Meliddu was besieged in 675 BC, but without success. That same year, Humban-Haltash II of Elam began a campaign 
1 CONC against Sippar, but was defeated by the Babylonians, and died soon afterwards. His brother and successor Urtaki restored peace with Assyria.
1 CONT
1 CONT A preliminary campaign against Egypt begun by Esarhaddon the next year seems to have failed. Meanwhile, Esarhaddon was waging war in the land of Bazu, situated opposite of the island of Dilmun (Bahrain), probably Qatar, "where sna
1 CONC kes and scorpions cover the ground like ants" - a dry land of salt deserts. In 673 BC, Esarhaddon waged war against Urartu under king Rusas II, that had strengthened again after the ravages of Sargon and the Cimmerians.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 672 BC, crown prince Sin-iddina-apla died. He had been the oldest son and designated as king of Assyria, while the second son Shamash-shum-ukin was to become the ruler of Babylon. Now, the younger Assurbanipal became crown prin
1 CONC ce, but he was very unpopular with the court and the priesthood. Contracts were made with leading Assyrians, members of the royal family and foreign rulers, to assure their loyalty to the crown prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 671 BC Esarhaddon went to war against Pharaoh Taharqa of Egypt. Part of his army stayed behind to deal with rebellions in Tyre, and perhaps Ashkelon. The remainder went south to Rapihu, then crossed the Sinai, a desert inhabite
1 CONC d by dreadful and dangerous animals, and entered Egypt. In the summer he took Memphis, and Taharqa fled to Upper Egypt. Esarhaddon now called himself "king of Musur, Patros and Kush", and returned with rich booty from the cities o
1 CONC f the delta. Almost as soon as the king left, Egypt rebelled against Assyrian rule.
1 CONT
1 CONT Esarhaddon had to contend with court intrigues at Nineveh that led to the execution of several nobles, and sent his general, Sha-Nabu-shu, to restore order in the Nile Valley. In 669 BC, he went to Egypt in person, but suddenly di
1 CONC ed in autumn of the same year, in Harran. He was succeeded by Assurbanipal as king of Assyria and Shamash-shum-ukin as king of Babylonia.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT In fiction
1 CONT Esarhaddon is also a major character in the novel The Assyrian, written as a narrative of a fictional half-brother to Esarhaddon, "Tiglath".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External links
1 CONT A summary of Assyrian kings 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sennacherib King of Assyria
1 CONT 681–669 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ashurbanipal 
1 CONT King of Babylon
1 CONT 681–669 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Shamash-shum-ukin 
0 @N6000000001503906268@ NOTE The Assyrians ruled Upper Asia[110] for five hundred and twenty years,
1 CONT
1 CONT and from them the Medes were the first who made revolt. These having
1 CONT
1 CONT fought for their freedom with the Assyrians proved themselves good
1 CONT
1 CONT men, and thus they pushed off the yoke of slavery from themselves and
1 CONT
1 CONT were set free; and after them the other nations also did the same as
1 CONT
1 CONT the Medes: and when all on the continent were thus independent, they
1 CONT
1 CONT returned again to despotic rule as follows:--96. There appeared among
1 CONT
1 CONT the Medes a man of great ability whose name was Deïokes, and this man
1 CONT
1 CONT was the son of Phraortes. This Deïokes, having formed a desire for
1 CONT
1 CONT despotic power, did thus:--whereas the Medes dwelt in separate
1 CONT
1 CONT villages, he, being even before that time of great repute in his own
1 CONT
1 CONT village, set himself to practise just dealing much more and with
1 CONT
1 CONT greater zeal than before; and this he did although there was much
1 CONT
1 CONT lawlessness throughout the whole of Media, and although he knew that
1 CONT
1 CONT injustice is ever at feud with justice. And the Medes of the same
1 CONT
1 CONT village, seeing his manners, chose him for their judge. So he, since
1 CONT
1 CONT he was aiming at power, was upright and just, and doing thus he had no
1 CONT
1 CONT little praise from his fellow-citizens, insomuch that those of the
1 CONT
1 CONT other villages learning that Deïokes was a man who more than all
1 CONT
1 CONT others gave decision rightly, whereas before this they had been wont
1 CONT
1 CONT to suffer from unjust judgments, themselves also when they heard it
1 CONT
1 CONT came gladly to Deïokes to have their causes determined, and at last
1 CONT
1 CONT they trusted the business to no one else. 97. Then, as more and more
1 CONT
1 CONT continually kept coming to him, because men learnt that his decisions
1 CONT
1 CONT proved to be according to the truth, Deïokes perceiving that
1 CONT
1 CONT everything was referred to himself would no longer sit in the place
1 CONT
1 CONT where he used formerly to sit in public to determine causes, and said
1 CONT
1 CONT that he would determine causes no more, for it was not profitable for
1 CONT
1 CONT him to neglect his own affairs and to determine causes for his
1 CONT
1 CONT neighbours all through the day. So then, since robbery and lawlessness
1 CONT
1 CONT prevailed even much more in the villages than they did before, the
1 CONT
1 CONT Medes having assembled together in one place considered with one
1 CONT
1 CONT another and spoke about the state in which they were: and I suppose
1 CONT
1 CONT the friends of Deïokes spoke much to this effect: "Seeing that we are
1 CONT
1 CONT not able to dwell in the land under the present order of things, let
1 CONT
1 CONT us set up a king from among ourselves, and thus the land will be well
1 CONT
1 CONT governed and we ourselves shall turn to labour, and shall not be
1 CONT
1 CONT ruined by lawlessness." By some such words as these they persuaded
1 CONT
1 CONT themselves to have a king. 98. And when they straightway proposed the
1 CONT
1 CONT question whom they should set up to be king, Deïokes was much put
1 CONT
1 CONT forward and commended by every one, until at last they agreed that he
1 CONT
1 CONT should be their king. And he bade them build for him a palace worthy
1 CONT
1 CONT of the royal dignity and strengthen him with a guard of spearmen. And
1 CONT
1 CONT the Medes did so: for they built him a large and strong palace in that
1 CONT
1 CONT part of the land which he told them, and they allowed him to select
1 CONT
1 CONT spearmen from all the Medes. And when he had obtained the rule over
1 CONT
1 CONT them, he compelled the Medes to make one fortified city and pay chief
1 CONT
1 CONT attention to this, having less regard to the other cities. And as the
1 CONT
1 CONT Medes obeyed him in this also, he built large and strong walls, those
1 CONT
1 CONT which are now called Agbatana, standing in circles one within the
1 CONT
1 CONT other. And this wall is so contrived that one circle is higher than
1 CONT
1 CONT the next by the height of the battlements alone. And to some extent, I
1 CONT
1 CONT suppose, the nature of the ground, seeing that it is on a hill,
1 CONT
1 CONT assists towards this end; but much more was it produced by art, since
1 CONT
1 CONT the circles are in all seven in number.[111] And within the last
1 CONT
1 CONT circle are the royal palace and the treasure-houses. The largest of
1 CONT
1 CONT these walls is in size about equal to the circuit of the wall round
1 CONT
1 CONT Athens; and of the first circle the battlements are white, of the
1 CONT
1 CONT second black, of the third crimson, of the fourth blue, of the fifth
1 CONT
1 CONT red: thus are the battlements of all the circles coloured with various
1 CONT
1 CONT tints, and the two last have their battlements one of them overlaid
1 CONT
1 CONT with silver and the other with gold. 99. These walls then Deïokes
1 CONT
1 CONT built for himself and round his own palace, and the people he
1 CONT
1 CONT commanded to dwell round about the wall. And after all was built,
1 CONT
1 CONT Deïokes established the rule, which he was the first to establish,
1 CONT
1 CONT ordaining that none should enter into the presence of the king, but
1 CONT
1 CONT that they deal with him always through messengers; and that the king
1 CONT
1 CONT should be seen by no one; and moreover that to laugh or to spit in
1 CONT
1 CONT presence is unseemly, and this last for every one without
1 CONT
1 CONT exception.[112] Now he surrounded himself with this state[113] to the
1 CONT
1 CONT end that his fellows, who had been brought up with him and were of no
1 CONT
1 CONT meaner family nor behind him in manly virtue, might not be grieved by
1 CONT
1 CONT seeing him and make plots against him, but that being unseen by them
1 CONT
1 CONT he might be thought to be of different mould. 100. Having set these
1 CONT
1 CONT things in order and strengthened himself in his despotism, he was
1 CONT
1 CONT severe in preserving justice; and the people used to write down their
1 CONT
1 CONT causes and send them in to his presence, and he determined the
1 CONT
1 CONT questions which were brought in to him and sent them out again. Thus
1 CONT
1 CONT he used to do about the judgment of causes; and he also took order for
1 CONT
1 CONT this, that is to say, if he heard that any one was behaving in an
1 CONT
1 CONT unruly manner, he sent for him and punished him according as each act
1 CONT
1 CONT of wrong deserved, and he had watchers and listeners about all the
1 CONT
1 CONT land over which he ruled.
1 CONT
1 CONT 101. Deïokes then united the Median race alone, and was ruler of this:
1 CONT
1 CONT and of the Medes there are the tribes which here follow, namely,
1 CONT
1 CONT Busai, Paretakenians, Struchates, Arizantians, Budians, Magians: the
1 CONT
1 CONT tribes of the Medes are so many in number. 102. Now the son of Deïokes
1 CONT
1 CONT was Phraortes, who when Deïokes was dead, having been king for three-
1 CONT
1 CONT and-fifty years,
0 @N6000000002187613920@ NOTE Deioces
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Deioces, Déjocès or Diyako (709 to 656 B.C.) was the first Aryan king of the Medes, an Iranian people in what would become Iran. He united seven Median tribes and became their judge and leader, beginning in 701 BC. After seven yea
1 CONC rs of rule he resigned, the Medes elected him as king until 665 BC. Deioces built a palace in the capital, Ecbatana, now known as Hamadan. The story of his rise to power is told in book one of The History by Herodotus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT — King of Persia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Phraortes 
